WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
A strawberry and a person use the same kind of DNA.

2
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.740
What inside that molecule can help make them different?

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.180
Both lead into cells, then toward the same twisting ladder.

4
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.220
The puzzle is where differences sit.

5
00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:23.370
DNA is a double helix, like a ladder twisted along its length.

6
00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:26.610
Sugar and phosphate form both sides.

7
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.190
Those side parts are the same kinds in every living thing.

8
00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:35.470
The useful difference is not the frame around the rungs.

9
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:38.133
The rungs carry base pairs.

10
00:00:39.473 --> 00:00:46.493
Each base has a letter name: A, T, C, or G. In ordinary double-stranded DNA, A pairs

11
00:00:46.493 --> 00:00:53.533
with T. C pairs with G. Pairing lets either side tell us the partner across each

12
00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:54.533
rung.

13
00:00:54.917 --> 00:00:57.097
Two ladders have matching blue sides.

14
00:00:58.457 --> 00:01:00.697
Their rung letters appear in different orders.

15
00:01:02.057 --> 00:01:06.557
Which part can carry different instructions: the shared sides or the changing base

16
00:01:06.557 --> 00:01:11.657
order? Hold your answer while a small reader waits at the first rung.

17
00:01:17.533 --> 00:01:19.873
The changing order carries information.

18
00:01:21.593 --> 00:01:23.333
Sequence means base order along DNA.

19
00:01:25.033 --> 00:01:26.713
Traits are features of living things.

20
00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:30.073
Here is the rule connecting both films.

21
00:01:31.513 --> 00:01:36.833
DNA sequence helps shape traits; DNA amount does not rank organism complexity.

22
00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:39.973
This film builds the sequence half.

23
00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:44.933
A highlighted sequence sits within a gene, a section of DNA.

24
00:01:46.393 --> 00:01:50.093
A chromosome is packaged DNA and carries many genes.

25
00:01:51.433 --> 00:01:54.533
Many genes carry instructions used to build proteins.

26
00:01:56.013 --> 00:01:58.293
Other genes carry code used in other ways.

27
00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:00.927
Follow one pea example.

28
00:02:02.247 --> 00:02:05.467
A working protein is needed to make purple flower colour.

29
00:02:06.707 --> 00:02:09.767
A change in its gene can stop that protein working.

30
00:02:10.987 --> 00:02:12.367
The flower can then be white.

31
00:02:13.547 --> 00:02:18.427
This case connects gene sequence, protein, and trait without making every trait

32
00:02:18.427 --> 00:02:19.427
simple.

33
00:02:19.817 --> 00:02:24.217
The path is a first model, not a promise that each gene makes one trait.

34
00:02:25.557 --> 00:02:27.477
Many traits depend on several genes.

35
00:02:28.957 --> 00:02:30.297
Surroundings can matter too.

36
00:02:31.657 --> 00:02:36.957
Some base changes alter nothing you can see, so scientists test changes instead of

37
00:02:36.957 --> 00:02:38.317
guessing from letters alone.

38
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:44.060
Almost every skin and muscle cell in one person carries the same DNA.

39
00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.640
Yet each cell type uses different genes.

40
00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.300
Different proteins follow, so the cells look and work differently.

41
00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.460
The code matters together with how each cell uses it.

42
00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:00.690
Follow base order through a gene, protein, and trait.

43
00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:06.490
Shared parts can carry different sequences, and cells can use genes differently.

44
00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:09.750
So DNA sequence helps shape traits.

45
00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:15.130
Counting whole chromosome sets is a different question, for the second film.
